  "body": " The next digit  Write . It takes a moment, and there is one third, comfortably on the page. Now try to replace the dots with what they stand for. The pencil will wear down. The hand will complain. The threes will remain entirely unaffected by these developments.  We usually let the dots do their job without much gratitude. They allow a finished piece of writing to refer to an unfinished piece of work. With these particular dots, we even know what the work is: another three, then another. Ask for the millionth digit and we can answer without writing our way there. Our modest fraction has arranged quite a lot of the future.  Long division makes such arrangements every day. Two integers supply enough information to find the next decimal place, then the next. We learn the procedure at school, finish the assigned problems, and move on. The procedure does not share our interest in finishing. Even gives it an inexhaustible occupation.  There are decimals for which division of ordinary integers will never supply the digits. A short set of directions can escape every fraction; later we will make such an escape with marks on graph paper. Then someone else will take the pencil between our turns. They may add one digit or a thousand, provided they eventually hand it back. The decimal becomes a shared undertaking, and a question about numbers becomes a game about what one person can still guarantee when another has a say.  René Baire’s mathematics will give us room to play. At Princeton, Alonzo Church and three of his students—Turing, Kleene, and Henkin—will supply different ways of looking at the directions, the proofs about them, and the worlds those proofs describe. Their work did not begin as a single project, and they will enter our story at different times. What joins them here is a stubbornly small request: another decimal digit, please.  At the far end of the argument, two fixed programs will bring us back to long division. In a suitable mathematical world, they finish with integers whose quotient follows every ordinary finite beginning of a chosen decimal—even a decimal no ordinary program can print. The same programs work for different choices by living in different worlds. The construction depends on what our proof system can express and verify; we will build those requirements into the journey rather than ask the decimal to perform a miracle.  Nothing we do will make an ordinary calculation change an answer it has already reached. The trouble is stranger: a world can regard a search as having ended when no ordinary number of steps will get us to its ending. To understand how, we will need to look closely at what counts as a calculation and what counts as a proof. The pencil has more work ahead of it than the threes initially suggested.  "
